          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does stopping actually change anything at this stage?",
          "answer": "Yes, according to the COPD guidance I found. The document \"guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management\" says that smoking cessation is the only intervention shown to change the rate at which lung function declines. In other words, stopping is the one step shown to slow that decline.\n\nThe same document says that pulmonary rehabilitation (a structured exercise and education programme) improves breathlessness and exercise ability more than any inhaler does. It also says that vaccines against influenza, pneumococcal disease, COVID-19 and, where recommended, RSV reduce flare-ups.\n\nThe results did not say how much difference quitting makes at your particular stage, or how much lung function you might keep by stopping now. Your clinician can talk about that with you, using your test results.\n\nThe heart-disease and blood-pressure documents also say smoking cessation is important, but they are about other conditions, so I have not used them to answer your question.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
